WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.016
Two support agents are typing into the same note at the same moment.

2
00:00:07.233 --> 00:00:14.003
Each one's typing shows up at once on their own laptop, and there's no server in the middle deciding who went first.

3
00:00:14.233 --> 00:00:19.475
At the end, both laptops must show the same note, with both people's typing kept.

4
00:00:19.475 --> 00:00:21.223
How, with no one in charge?

5
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:28.037
Start with the simplest way to send an edit: insert a character at a position, counting from 0.

6
00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:32.475
Agent a types a dollar sign before the 4: insert dollar at 4.

7
00:00:32.475 --> 00:00:36.716
Agent b types an exclamation mark at the end: insert it at 6.

8
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.784
Each sends the edit to the other.

9
00:00:40.784 --> 00:00:43.844
b's copy becomes pay $40!.

10
00:00:43.844 --> 00:00:46.904
a's copy becomes pay $4!0.

11
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.993
Position 6 meant after the 0 in b's text.

12
00:00:49.993 --> 00:00:52.956
In a's text, it's in the middle of the 40.

13
00:00:52.956 --> 00:00:55.355
Same number, two different places.

14
00:00:55.533 --> 00:01:01.680
One fix moves the positions before applying them, and it usually needs a server to agree the order.

15
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.784
What if an edit never mentioned a position at all?

16
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.231
Picture a printed page with a small serial number under every letter.

17
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:14.923
Instead of put this at position 5, each person writes put this after letter 4a.

18
00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:19.321
Anyone can apply the notes in any order, and get the same page.

19
00:01:19.321 --> 00:01:23.443
The serial number is the character's id, and it never changes.

20
00:01:23.443 --> 00:01:24.839
The note is the edit.

21
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:35.183
This is a CRDT, a conflict-free replicated data type: a data type whose copies can take edits apart, and still merge to the same result.

22
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.832
In the lesson's version, an id is a counter plus a site name.

23
00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:43.392
Site a typed pay 40, so its letters are 1a to 6a.

24
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:48.680
Each site's next counter is one more than the highest it has seen from anyone.

25
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.331
So a letter typed after another always has a bigger counter.

26
00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:54.983
Now the same two edits, by name.

27
00:01:54.983 --> 00:01:59.117
a sends: insert dollar, named 7a, after 4a, the space.

28
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:04.342
b sends: insert exclamation mark, named 7b, after 6a, the 0.

29
00:02:04.342 --> 00:02:06.695
Neither mentions a position.

30
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.677
What will each copy show?

31
00:02:08.677 --> 00:02:09.815
Pause and guess.

32
00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:16.145
Both say pay $40!.

33
00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:22.645
The dollar finds the space wherever the space has moved to, and the exclamation mark finds the 0.

34
00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:24.635
Nobody had to be in charge.

35
00:02:24.635 --> 00:02:27.189
Each edit carries the name it hangs on.

36
00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:28.690
Two more rules.

37
00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:34.080
When two inserts name the same letter, the bigger id goes first, on every site.

38
00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.247
And deletes.

39
00:02:35.247 --> 00:02:37.928
On paper you'd rub the letter out.

40
00:02:37.928 --> 00:02:39.269
Here that breaks.

41
00:02:39.433 --> 00:02:44.261
Say a deletes the 0, while b types a 5 right after it, named 8b.

42
00:02:44.433 --> 00:02:46.179
b's note says after 6a.

43
00:02:46.179 --> 00:02:49.823
If 6a were gone, b's 5 would have nowhere to go.

44
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.822
So a delete only marks its letter as gone, and keeps the name.

45
00:02:53.822 --> 00:02:55.795
That mark is called a tombstone.

46
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.136
The letter is dead, but it still gets mail.

47
00:03:00.136 --> 00:03:02.925
Both sites end with pay $45!.

48
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.100
The price is extra data, and it only grows.

49
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:17.685
In the lesson's test, 10,000 random edits leave 2,136 letters you can see, and 6,068 kept, one for every letter ever typed.

50
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:21.893
That's 17 bytes of names and marks for each visible letter.

51
00:03:21.893 --> 00:03:23.652
A plain string needs none.

52
00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:33.534
Real libraries carry more: in Automerge 2, a CRDT library, pasting a long novel took 700 MB of memory.

53
00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:37.704
Automerge 3 takes 1.3 MB for the same paste.

54
00:03:38.067 --> 00:03:39.490
And one more limit.

55
00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:45.483
A CRDT promises that the copies agree, not that the result is what anyone meant.

56
00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:51.112
Two agents who fix the same typo at once can end up with the fix typed twice.

57
00:03:51.112 --> 00:03:53.009
Agreed, and wrong together.

58
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.595
So, how do two copies merge with no one in charge?

59
00:03:56.595 --> 00:03:58.768
Name every letter when it's typed.

60
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.879
Insert after a name, never at a position.

61
00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:04.688
Keep the names, even of deleted letters.

62
00:04:05.067 --> 00:04:06.341
One quick check.

63
00:04:06.341 --> 00:04:10.005
Site b receives: insert x, named 9a, after 8a.

64
00:04:10.005 --> 00:04:11.916
But b has never seen 8a.

65
00:04:11.916 --> 00:04:14.386
Should it apply the insert now?

66
00:04:14.386 --> 00:04:15.660
Pause and think.

67
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:19.138
No.

68
00:04:19.138 --> 00:04:21.201
It waits until 8a arrives.

69
00:04:21.201 --> 00:04:25.405
An edit can only hang on a name the site already has.
